    "problemStatement": "New Member Orientation doubles as a party leadership event",
    "problemDescription": "New Member Orientation is currently intertwined with party leadership events, so incoming members' first structured experience of Congress emphasizes party organization over the civility, collaboration, and leadership skills they will need to legislate. Moving orientation to a bipartisan body, on its own, does not specify what orientation should teach.",
    "solutionStatement": "Add a civility and collaboration curriculum, held apart from leadership events",
    "solutionDescription": "Hold New Member Orientation separately from party leadership events and build its curriculum around civility, collaboration, and leadership skills rather than party organization. The bipartisan body running orientation designs and delivers this content each term.",
    "horizonJustification": "It changes what orientation teaches and where it sits on the calendar, but leaves orientation as a single term opening event rather than a standing, term long program.",
